        {
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "code": "function identity<T>(arg: T): T {\n  return arg;\n}",
          "q": "What does the `<T>` signify in this TypeScript function?",
          "options": [
            "It declares a Type Variable (Generic), allowing the function to accept and return any type while maintaining strict type safety",
            "It is a syntax error in JavaScript",
            "It casts the argument to a string",
            "It means the function returns a Promise"
          ],
          "a": "It declares a Type Variable (Generic), allowing the function to accept and return any type while maintaining strict type safety",
          "explanation": "Generics allow you to write reusable components that work with a variety of types rather than a single one, while still giving you perfect autocomplete."
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
