            "Generics automatically mock the API response",
            "Generics make the API request faster",
            "Generics bypass CORS errors"
          ],
          "a": "You can pass the expected Interface as a Generic type parameter, so the returned data is correctly typed",
          "explanation": "By calling `fetchData<UserResponse>()`, TypeScript knows the result matches the UserResponse shape."
        }
      ],
      "Medium": [
        {
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "code": "interface ResponseData<DataShape> {\n  status: number;\n  payload: DataShape;\n}",
          "q": "How would you correctly instantiate this type for an API returning a `string` payload?",
          "options": [
            "let res: ResponseData<string>;",
            "let res: ResponseData(string);",
            "let res: ResponseData = string;",
            "let res<string>: ResponseData;"
          ],
          "a": "let res: ResponseData<string>;",
          "explanation": "You pass the type argument inside angle brackets `< >` when using the generic interface."
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "scenario": "A developer writes a generic function but TypeScript complains because they try to call `arg.length`, and TypeScript doesn't know if the generic `T` has a length property.",
          "q": "How can you fix this using Generic Constraints?",
          "options": [
            "Change `<T>` to `<T extends { length: number }>`",
            "Cast arg to `any`",
            "Change the generic to `<T = length>`",
            "Remove the generic entirely"
          ],
          "a": "Change `<T>` to `<T extends { length: number }>`",
          "explanation": "The `extends` keyword in a generic declaration enforces a constraint, ensuring the passed type meets specific minimum requirements (like having a length property)."
        }
      ],
      "Hard": [
        {
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "code": "const getProperty = <T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K) => {\n  return obj[key];\n};",
          "q": "What sophisticated type-safety mechanism is being used here?",
          "options": [
            "The compiler is enforcing that the requested 'key' strictly exists as a property name on the generic object 'T'",
            "It is converting the object into an array of keys",
            "It is making the property optional",
            "It is a runtime validation check"
          ],
          "a": "The compiler is enforcing that the requested 'key' strictly exists as a property name on the generic object 'T'",
          "explanation": "Using `K extends keyof T` is an advanced TypeScript pattern that guarantees you cannot ask for a property that doesn't exist on the object, preventing undefined errors at compile time."
        }
      ]
    },
    "icon": "🌱",
