          "a": [
            "npx playwright test --config=packages/e2e/playwright.config.ts",
            "cd packages/e2e && npx playwright test"
          ],
          "explanation": "You must either navigate to the directory where the config lives, or explicitly tell Playwright where the config is using the `--config` flag. Passing the folder path (`packages/e2e/`) tells it where the tests are, but it will still look for the config in the root unless specified."
        },
        {
          "objective": "cli_config",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "scenario": "Your enterprise requires testing against three environments: QA, Staging, and Production. You want developers to easily run `ENV=qa npx playwright test` without modifying the config file.",
          "q": "How should you design the `playwright.config.ts` to dynamically resolve the `baseURL` based on the `ENV` variable?",
          "options": [
            "Create a dictionary mapping `{ qa: 'url1', staging: 'url2' }` and assign `baseURL: urls[process.env.ENV || 'qa']`.",
            "Create three separate playwright.config files and use bash scripts to swap them out.",
            "Use the CLI flag `--base-url=$ENV`.",
            "Export a default async function that reads the database to get the URL."
          ],
          "a": "Create a dictionary mapping `{ qa: 'url1', staging: 'url2' }` and assign `baseURL: urls[process.env.ENV || 'qa']`.",
          "explanation": "Dynamically resolving environment variables using a dictionary map inside `playwright.config.ts` is the standard enterprise pattern. It keeps configuration centralized, type-safe, and avoids the maintenance nightmare of managing multiple config files."
        },
        {
          "objective": "cli_config",
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "code": "export default defineConfig({\n  projects: [\n    {\n      name: 'setup',\n      testMatch: /.*\\.setup\\.ts/,\n    },\n    {\n      name: 'chromium',\n      use: { ...devices['Desktop Chrome'] },\n      dependencies: ['setup'],\n    }\n  ]\n});",
          "q": "What is the primary architectural advantage of this 'Project Dependencies' pattern over using a `globalSetup` script?",
          "options": [
            "It allows the setup script to utilize the full Playwright Test runner (fixtures, reporting, tracing) instead of a raw Node.js script.",
            "It allows the setup script to run on a different CI server.",
            "It prevents Playwright from running the setup script more than once globally.",
            "It converts the setup script into a GitHub Action automatically."
          ],
          "a": "It allows the setup script to utilize the full Playwright Test runner (fixtures, reporting, tracing) instead of a raw Node.js script.",
          "explanation": "`globalSetup` runs outside the Playwright Test runner, meaning you lose built-in fixtures (like `page`, `request`), traces, video recording, and HTML reporting. Using project dependencies runs your setup as a native test, giving you full debugging and reporting capabilities if the setup fails."
        },
        {
          "objective": "cli_config",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "scenario": "You configure Playwright to generate an HTML report, but you notice that the report is silently overwritten every time you run a different shard on your CI nodes. You need to combine the reports at the end of the pipeline.",
          "q": "Which configuration/flag pattern is REQUIRED to successfully merge sharded Playwright reports in a CI environment?",
          "options": [
            "Set the reporter to `['blob']` on the CI shards, then run `npx playwright merge-reports` in a final job.",
            "Set the reporter to `['html']` and use `--merge` on the shards.",
            "Configure the HTML reporter to append using `{ append: true }`.",
            "Zip the `playwright-report` folders from all shards and extract them into the same directory."
          ],
          "a": "Set the reporter to `['blob']` on the CI shards, then run `npx playwright merge-reports` in a final job.",
          "explanation": "HTML reports cannot be cleanly appended or zipped together because of embedded assets and state. Playwright introduced the `blob` reporter specifically for sharding. Shards output `.zip` blob files, which a final CI job collects and merges into a unified HTML report using `npx playwright merge-reports`."
        }
      ]
    },
