          "difficulty": "hard",
          "code": "// File: /components/Sidebar.ts\nimport { DashboardPage } from '../pages/DashboardPage';\n\nexport class Sidebar {\n  async clickHome() {\n    await this.page.click('#home');\n    return new DashboardPage(this.page);\n  }\n}",
          "q": "This code results in an architectural anti-pattern known as a Circular Dependency. Why?",
          "options": [
            "A Component (`Sidebar`) is importing and returning a Page (`DashboardPage`), but Pages generally import Components, creating an infinite resolution loop.",
            "The Component is missing the `expect` library.",
            "Components should not return any objects.",
            "A Component should never interact with the `page` object directly."
          ],
          "a": "A Component (`Sidebar`) is importing and returning a Page (`DashboardPage`), but Pages generally import Components, creating an infinite resolution loop.",
          "explanation": "Dependency direction must strictly flow downward: Tests -> Pages -> Components. If a Component imports a Page, and the Page imports the Component, they become tightly coupled and Node.js circular dependency errors can occur. Methods in lower layers should return `void` and let the test handle instantiation of the next page."
        },
