          "q": "Why would an engineer manually inject the `browser` fixture and instantiate a `context` like this, instead of just using the default `page` fixture?",
          "options": [
            "To override the global viewport size or permissions for this specific test only.",
            "Because the default `page` fixture does not have a viewport.",
            "Because `browser.newContext()` runs much faster.",
            "Because it is required for TypeScript."
          ],
          "a": "To override the global viewport size or permissions for this specific test only.",
          "explanation": "The default `page` fixture uses the settings defined in `playwright.config.ts`. If you need to test a specific edge case (like simulating a tiny mobile viewport or granting Geolocation permissions) just for one test, you build a custom context manually from the `browser` object."
        },
        {
          "objective": "browser_context",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "scenario": "You are testing a chat application. You need User A to send a message to User B, and you need to verify both sides of the conversation in real time.",
          "q": "What is the most efficient Playwright pattern to achieve this within a single test?",
          "options": [
            "Instantiate two separate `BrowserContext` objects (`contextA` and `contextB`), log in separately on each, and alternate commands.",
            "Open two tabs (`page1` and `page2`) in the same context.",
            "Launch two completely different browser types (Chromium and Firefox).",
            "You must write two separate tests."
          ],
          "a": "Instantiate two separate `BrowserContext` objects (`contextA` and `contextB`), log in separately on each, and alternate commands.",
          "explanation": "Because contexts are perfectly isolated, you can have 'User A' logged into `contextA` and 'User B' logged into `contextB` simultaneously within the same test script. If you used two tabs in the *same* context, the session (cookies) would be shared, causing a conflict."
        }
