  {
    "id": "52-locators-code",
    "title": "10.1: Advanced Locator Composition",
    "url": "courses/playwright/52-locators-code",
    "group": "🗝️ MODULE 10: ADVANCED LOCATORS",
    "level": 8,
    "weight": 15,
    "meta": {
      "time": "25 mins",
      "type": "Code",
      "outcome": "Isolate elements using complex filtering logic"
    },
    "objectives": [
      {
        "id": "filter_mastery",
        "title": "Use .filter() and .hasText() like a pro"
      }
    ],
    "tasks": [
      {
        "id": "write_filter",
        "title": "Complex Filter",
        "hint": "Use locator().filter()",
        "rules": [
          {
            "type": "contains",
            "value": "filter"
          },
          {
            "type": "contains",
            "value": "hasText"
          },
          {
            "type": "ast_forbidden_callee",
            "value": "page.waitForTimeout"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "quizMapping": [
      {
        "objective": "filter_mastery",
        "weight": 100
      }
    ],
    "spec": {
      "method": ".filter()",
      "attribute": "hasText / has"
    },
    "quizPool": {
      "Easy": [
        {
          "objective": "advanced_filtering",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "q": "How can you dynamically narrow down this massive list of buttons to find the exact target?",
          "options": [
            "To format the text output of the locator.",
            "To narrow down a broad locator (like matching 10 rows) into a more specific subset (like the 1 row that contains specific text), without relying on brittle index numbers.",
            "To remove elements from the DOM.",
            "To filter out invisible elements automatically."
          ],
          "a": "To narrow down a broad locator (like matching 10 rows) into a more specific subset (like the 1 row that contains specific text), without relying on brittle index numbers.",
          "explanation": "Filtering allows you to start with a broad, semantic query (`getByRole('listitem')`) and mathematically narrow it down by state or child content (`filter({ hasText: 'Apples' })`) to achieve strict uniqueness.",
          "scenario": "Your locator `page.getByRole('button')` finds 10 buttons. You only want the one that sits inside the '.checkout-modal' container and contains the text 'Pay'."
        },
        {
          "objective": "advanced_filtering",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "scenario": "You have a list of 5 product cards. You want to click the 'Add to Cart' button specifically inside the card titled 'Laptop'.",
          "q": "Which code snippet correctly demonstrates robust locator chaining?",
          "options": [
            "page.getByText('Laptop').getByRole('button', { name: 'Add to Cart' }).click()",
            "page.locator('.card').filter({ hasText: 'Laptop' }).getByRole('button', { name: 'Add to Cart' }).click()",
            "page.locator('.card:nth-child(2) > button').click()",
            "page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Add to Cart' }).filter({ hasText: 'Laptop' }).click()"
          ],
          "a": "page.locator('.card').filter({ hasText: 'Laptop' }).getByRole('button', { name: 'Add to Cart' }).click()",
          "explanation": "Chaining works from the outside in. First, you grab all product cards. Second, you filter them down to the specific card containing the text 'Laptop'. Finally, you scope inside that specific card to find the 'Add to Cart' button. This architecture survives re-ordering of the cards."
        },
        {
          "objective": "advanced_filtering",
          "type": "multi-select",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "scenario": "Your locator `page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Delete' })` throws a Strict Mode error because there are 3 Delete buttons on the page.",
          "q": "Which of the following methods are technically valid ways to bypass the error and force Playwright to interact with a specific element in the array? (Select all that apply)",
          "options": [
            "locator.first()",
            "locator.last()",
            "locator.nth(1)",
            "locator.filter()",
            "locator.random()"
          ],
          "a": [
            "locator.first()",
            "locator.last()",
            "locator.nth(1)",
            "locator.filter()"
          ],
          "explanation": "Playwright provides structural index methods (`first`, `last`, `nth`) to break strict mode ties. It also provides semantic narrowing via `filter`. There is no `random()` method."
        },
        {
          "objective": "advanced_filtering",
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "code": "const row = page.locator('tr').filter({ hasText: 'Alice' });\nawait row.click();",
          "q": "If the table contains two rows, one with text 'Alice' and one with text 'AliceSmith', what will happen when `row.click()` executes?",
          "options": [
            "It clicks the first row.",
            "It clicks both rows simultaneously.",
            "It throws a Strict Mode violation (`resolved to 2 elements`) because `hasText` performs a substring match by default, meaning both 'Alice' and 'AliceSmith' pass the filter.",
            "It fails because `tr` is an invalid locator."
          ],
          "a": "It throws a Strict Mode violation (`resolved to 2 elements`) because `hasText` performs a substring match by default, meaning both 'Alice' and 'AliceSmith' pass the filter.",
          "explanation": "The `hasText` filter is a substring match. To enforce exact matching, you must pass a regular expression anchored with bounds (e.g., `hasText: /^Alice$/`), or use a stricter selector."
        },
        {
          "objective": "advanced_filtering",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "scenario": "You need to find a button that possesses TWO specific attributes: It must have the role 'button', and it must contain the title attribute 'Submit'.",
          "q": "How can you combine locators to enforce multiple conditions simultaneously?",
          "options": [
            "page.getByRole('button').or(page.getByTitle('Submit'))",
            "page.getByRole('button').and(page.getByTitle('Submit'))",
            "page.getByRole('button').concat(page.getByTitle('Submit'))",
            "page.locator('button || title=Submit')"
          ],
          "a": "page.getByRole('button').and(page.getByTitle('Submit'))",
          "explanation": "The `.and()` method intersects two locators. It returns only the DOM nodes that mathematically satisfy the conditions of both the left-hand and right-hand locators."
        }
      ],
      "Medium": [
        {
          "objective": "advanced_filtering",
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "code": "await page.locator('button').nth(3).click();",
          "q": "Why is using `.nth(3)` considered an architectural anti-pattern and highly discouraged in modern UI automation?",
          "options": [
            "It is extremely brittle. If a new button is added anywhere in the DOM before the target button, the index shifts, and the test will click the wrong element without throwing an error.",
            "It is deprecated.",
            "It requires zero-based indexing, so it clicks the 4th button, confusing developers.",
            "It disables auto-waiting."
          ],
          "a": "It is extremely brittle. If a new button is added anywhere in the DOM before the target button, the index shifts, and the test will click the wrong element without throwing an error.",
          "explanation": "Index-based locators (`first`, `last`, `nth`) rely purely on the physical DOM rendering order. They possess no semantic meaning. Using them guarantees test rot as the application evolves. SDETs must always strive to use semantic `filter({ hasText: ... })` instead."
        },
        {
          "objective": "advanced_filtering",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "scenario": "A dashboard has 10 widget containers. Only one of the widgets contains a `<canvas>` element used for rendering a chart.",
          "q": "How do you construct a locator that specifically targets the widget container that has a canvas inside it?",
          "options": [
            "page.locator('.widget > canvas')",
            "page.locator('canvas').closest('.widget')",
            "page.locator('.widget').filter({ has: page.locator('canvas') })",
            "page.locator('.widget').contains('canvas')"
          ],
          "a": "page.locator('.widget').filter({ has: page.locator('canvas') })",
          "explanation": "The `filter({ has: Locator })` method is incredibly powerful. It allows you to query a parent element based on the existence of a specific inner descendant. Unlike `.widget > canvas` (which returns the canvas), `filter({ has })` returns the parent `.widget`."
        },
        {
          "objective": "advanced_filtering",
          "type": "multi-select",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "scenario": "An application has two drastically different UI layouts for Mobile and Desktop. On Desktop, the save button is `getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' })`. On Mobile, the text is hidden, and it is just an icon: `getByLabel('Save Icon')`.",
          "q": "How can you write a single robust locator that automatically works across both viewports? (Select all that apply)",
          "options": [
            "Use the `.or()` method: `const save = page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' }).or(page.getByLabel('Save Icon'));`",
            "Use an IF statement: `if (isMobile) ... else ...`",
            "You must write two separate test files.",
            "Use XPath with an OR operator: `//button[text()='Save'] | //*[@aria-label='Save Icon']`"
          ],
          "a": [
            "Use the `.or()` method: `const save = page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' }).or(page.getByLabel('Save Icon'));`",
            "Use an IF statement: `if (isMobile) ... else ...`",
            "Use XPath with an OR operator: `//button[text()='Save'] | //*[@aria-label='Save Icon']`"
          ],
          "explanation": "The `.or()` method mathematically unions two locators. Playwright will concurrently search for both. If it finds either one, it proceeds. This elegantly solves A/B testing and responsive design locators without requiring complex control flow logic."
        },
        {
          "objective": "advanced_filtering",
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "code": "const rows = page.locator('table tr');\nconst activeRows = rows.filter({ hasNot: page.locator('.deleted') });\nawait expect(activeRows).toHaveCount(5);",
