          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "code": "page.on('popup', async popup => {\n  await popup.waitForLoadState();\n  console.log(await popup.title());\n});",
          "q": "A developer writes this global listener to automatically log the title of any popup that opens during a massive E2E flow. What severe architectural race condition does this create?",
          "options": [
            "The `popup` object cannot be logged.",
            "Unawaited floating Promises. The callback function is `async`, but `page.on` is a synchronous event emitter. Playwright fires the event, but Node.js does not `await` the callback. The test might finish and close the browser while `popup.waitForLoadState()` is still pending, causing fatal `Target Closed` exceptions.",
            "It blocks the main thread.",
            "It triggers strict mode."
          ],
          "a": "Unawaited floating Promises. The callback function is `async`, but `page.on` is a synchronous event emitter. Playwright fires the event, but Node.js does not `await` the callback. The test might finish and close the browser while `popup.waitForLoadState()` is still pending, causing fatal `Target Closed` exceptions.",
          "explanation": "This is a critical Node.js concept. `EventEmitter.on()` does not return a Promise. If you do asynchronous work inside an event listener without a top-level await orchestrating it, you leak floating promises. This is a primary source of \"random\" CI crashes at the end of tests."
        },
