    "spec": {
      "pattern": "4-Layer",
      "standard": "SOC (Separation of Concerns)"
    },
    "quizPool": {
      "Easy": [
        {
          "objective": "arch_layers",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "q": "Why does the '4-Layer Separation' pattern strictly forbid this approach?",
          "options": [
            "To make tests run faster by utilizing 4 CPU cores.",
            "To strictly separate Test Intent, State Management (Fixtures), DOM Interaction (Page Objects), and Network/API (Clients) into distinct domains, preventing fragile 'spaghetti code'.",
            "To force developers to write 4 tests for every feature.",
            "To bypass strict mode."
          ],
          "a": "To strictly separate Test Intent, State Management (Fixtures), DOM Interaction (Page Objects), and Network/API (Clients) into distinct domains, preventing fragile 'spaghetti code'.",
          "explanation": "Separation of Concerns is the bedrock of software engineering. When tests contain raw CSS selectors, API tokens, and business logic all in one 200-line function, the framework becomes unmaintainable. The 4-layer architecture isolates these responsibilities.",
          "scenario": "A junior SDET writes a single 300-line test file that contains database queries, API setup, UI clicks, and assertions all mixed together."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_layers",
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "code": "test('Login', async ({ page }) => {\n  await page.locator('#user').fill('admin');\n  await page.locator('#pass').fill('1234');\n  await page.locator('.btn-primary').click();\n  await expect(page.locator('.welcome')).toBeVisible();\n});",
          "q": "According to the 4-Layer architecture, which specific layer is this test violently violating?",
          "options": [
            "The Test Layer. Tests should not contain raw DOM interaction logic (CSS selectors or `click` events).",
            "The API Layer.",
            "The Fixture Layer.",
            "It is not violating any layer."
          ],
          "a": "The Test Layer. Tests should not contain raw DOM interaction logic (CSS selectors or `click` events).",
          "explanation": "A Test Layer should only define the 'What' (the business intent and assertions). By hardcoding `#user` and `.btn-primary` directly into the test file, the test has illegally absorbed the responsibilities of the Page Object layer (the 'How')."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_layers",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "scenario": "You need to automate a scenario where a user creates an account. This requires navigating to the page, interacting with the form, and reading an authorization token.",
          "q": "In the 4-Layer model, which layer is responsible for physically clicking the 'Submit' button?",
          "options": [
            "The Test Layer (`spec.ts`).",
            "The API Layer (`authClient.ts`).",
            "The Page Object Layer (`RegistrationPage.ts`).",
            "The Fixture Layer (`baseTest.ts`)."
          ],
          "a": "The Page Object Layer (`RegistrationPage.ts`).",
          "explanation": "The Page Object Layer acts as the translator. It abstracts the physical HTML DOM (locators, clicks, typing) and exposes clean, readable methods (`createAccount()`) to the higher layers."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_layers",
          "type": "multi-select",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "scenario": "You are explaining the responsibilities of the Fixture Layer to a junior engineer.",
          "q": "Which of the following tasks belong exclusively in the Fixture Layer (`test.extend`)? (Select all that apply)",
          "options": [
            "Asserting that the 'Welcome' message is visible.",
            "Instantiating the `RegistrationPage` class and injecting it into the test.",
            "Setting up the database state (e.g., creating a test user) before the test runs, and deleting it afterward.",
            "Clicking the login button."
          ],
          "a": [
            "Instantiating the `RegistrationPage` class and injecting it into the test.",
            "Setting up the database state (e.g., creating a test user) before the test runs, and deleting it afterward."
          ],
          "explanation": "The Fixture Layer is the 'Orchestrator'. It handles dependency injection (providing Page Objects to tests) and environment setup/teardown. It should never contain assertions or business logic."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_layers",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "scenario": "The application's login form CSS changes from `.btn-primary` to `.btn-submit`.",
          "q": "If your framework strictly adheres to the 4-Layer Separation, how many files should you have to modify to fix the 50 broken login tests?",
          "options": [
            "50 files (every test script).",
            "1 file (the `LoginPage.ts` Page Object).",
            "2 files (the Page Object and the Fixture).",
            "You must rewrite the API layer."
          ],
          "a": "1 file (the `LoginPage.ts` Page Object).",
          "explanation": "This is the ultimate proof of a healthy architecture. If a UI change requires updating more than one file, your abstraction is leaking. The Page Object layer acts as a firewall, protecting the 50 test files from CSS changes."
        }
      ],
      "Medium": [
        {
          "objective": "arch_layers",
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "code": "export class DashboardPage {\n  async verifyTitle() {\n    await expect(this.page).toHaveTitle('Dashboard');\n  }\n}",
          "q": "Why is embedding `expect()` assertions inside Page Object methods considered an architectural anti-pattern by many enterprise teams?",
          "options": [
            "Page objects cannot access the `expect` library.",
            "It violates the Separation of Concerns. Page Objects should be dumb interaction APIs. The Test Layer owns the business requirements. If a Page Object asserts, it steals the 'Test Intent' from the test file, making the Page Object highly rigid and difficult to reuse in negative testing scenarios.",
            "It slows down execution.",
            "It causes Strict Mode violations."
          ],
          "a": "It violates the Separation of Concerns. Page Objects should be dumb interaction APIs. The Test Layer owns the business requirements. If a Page Object asserts, it steals the 'Test Intent' from the test file, making the Page Object highly rigid and difficult to reuse in negative testing scenarios.",
          "explanation": "If `verifyTitle` throws an error, the test fails inside the Page Object. If you want to write a negative test (e.g., verifying you are *not* on the Dashboard), you cannot reuse this Page Object method. Assertions belong in the `.spec.ts` files."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_layers",
          "type": "multi-select",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "scenario": "You are building the API Client Layer for your framework.",
          "q": "What specific structural responsibilities does the API Layer handle? (Select all that apply)",
          "options": [
            "Managing Authorization Headers and Bearer tokens for `APIRequestContext`.",
            "Bypassing the UI to seed database test data rapidly.",
            "Asserting that the UI displays the data correctly.",
            "Executing JavaScript in the browser console."
          ],
          "a": [
            "Managing Authorization Headers and Bearer tokens for `APIRequestContext`.",
            "Bypassing the UI to seed database test data rapidly."
          ],
          "explanation": "The API layer interacts directly with the backend via `request.post()`. It is used to generate data (e.g., creating 50 orders instantly) so the UI tests don't waste 10 minutes creating them via the frontend. It has no knowledge of the UI or assertions."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_layers",
          "type": "scenario",
