    "objectives": [
      {
        "id": "leaky_abstraction",
        "title": "Detect UI logic bleeding into test specs"
      }
    ],
    "tasks": [
      {
        "id": "fix_leak",
        "title": "Leaky Logic Fix",
        "hint": "Remove .click() from spec, move to page",
        "rules": [
          {
            "type": "contains",
            "value": "await this."
          },
          {
            "type": "ast_forbidden_callee",
            "value": "page.waitForTimeout"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "quizMapping": [
      {
        "objective": "leaky_abstraction",
        "weight": 100
      }
    ],
    "spec": {
      "detection": "Manual Audit",
      "rule": "Page-only Actions"
    },
    "quizPool": {
      "Easy": [
        {
          "objective": "arch_debug",
          "type": "mcq",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "q": "In software architecture, what is a 'Leaky Abstraction'?",
          "options": [
            "A memory leak in the browser.",
            "An abstraction (like a Page Object) that fails to completely hide its underlying complexity, forcing the caller (the test script) to manage low-level details it shouldn't know about.",
            "A network intercept that leaks API tokens.",
            "A variable that is globally scoped."
          ],
          "a": "An abstraction (like a Page Object) that fails to completely hide its underlying complexity, forcing the caller (the test script) to manage low-level details it shouldn't know about.",
          "explanation": "The entire point of a Page Object is to act as a black box. The test says 'Login', and the box handles the rest. If the test has to say 'Login, wait 2 seconds, and press Escape to close the modal', the modal management has 'leaked' out of the box and contaminated the test script."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_debug",
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "code": "await loginPage.login('user', 'pass');\nawait page.locator('.modal-close').click();\nawait dashboardPage.verifyWelcome();",
          "q": "Why is the second line (`page.locator...`) clear evidence of a leaky abstraction?",
          "options": [
            "It uses `await`.",
            "The test script is directly interacting with the physical DOM (`.modal-close`). The fact that a modal appears after login is a UI implementation detail that should be handled internally by the `loginPage.login()` method.",
            "It doesn't use `expect`.",
            "It clicks too fast."
          ],
          "a": "The test script is directly interacting with the physical DOM (`.modal-close`). The fact that a modal appears after login is a UI implementation detail that should be handled internally by the `loginPage.login()` method.",
          "explanation": "If the developers remove the modal tomorrow, this test will fail because it's trying to click a close button that no longer exists. The test shouldn't know about the modal. The Page Object should handle all structural navigation to deliver a clean state to the next step."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_debug",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "scenario": "A new developer on your team submits a PR containing this test code: `await cartPage.addItem('Laptop'); await page.waitForTimeout(3000); await cartPage.checkout();`",
          "q": "What leaked from the `cartPage.addItem()` method that forced the developer to add a hardcoded timeout?",
          "options": [
            "The API token.",
            "The synchronization logic. The `addItem` method returned control to the test *before* the application had finished rendering the addition, forcing the test script to manually manage the wait state.",
            "The CSS selector.",
            "The browser context."
          ],
          "a": "The synchronization logic. The `addItem` method returned control to the test *before* the application had finished rendering the addition, forcing the test script to manually manage the wait state.",
          "explanation": "A robust Page Object method must guarantee state stabilization. When `addItem` resolves, the item *must* be fully added and the UI settled. Leaking synchronization responsibility to the test suite is the leading cause of flaky test suites."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_debug",
          "type": "multi-select",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "scenario": "You are auditing a suite of 50 tests. You notice that 40 of them start with `await page.goto('/dashboard'); await page.evaluate(() => localStorage.setItem('token', '123'));`.",
          "q": "What architectural layer is leaking its responsibilities into the Test Layer? (Select all that apply)",
          "options": [
            "The Fixture Layer.",
            "The API / Auth Layer.",
            "The Assertion Layer.",
            "The CSS Layer."
          ],
          "a": [
            "The Fixture Layer.",
            "The API / Auth Layer."
          ],
          "explanation": "Test scripts should not be managing `localStorage` or manually navigating to setup URLs. This is prerequisite environment configuration. This leaked logic should be absorbed by a custom fixture (e.g., `test('My Test', async ({ authenticatedPage }) => { ... })`)."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_debug",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "scenario": "A Page Object exposes a method: `async getTableRows(): Promise<Locator[]> { return this.page.locator('tr').all(); }`.",
          "q": "Why is returning an array of raw Locators considered a minor leak in abstraction?",
          "options": [
            "Arrays are not supported.",
            "It forces the test script to loop over the array and extract the text manually. A better abstraction would be `async getTableData(): Promise<string[]>` which extracts the data internally and returns clean business data to the test.",
            "Locators are synchronous.",
            "It causes strict mode violations."
          ],
          "a": "It forces the test script to loop over the array and extract the text manually. A better abstraction would be `async getTableData(): Promise<string[]>` which extracts the data internally and returns clean business data to the test.",
          "explanation": "The test shouldn't care about DOM nodes (`tr`). The test cares about data ('Alice', 'Bob'). By extracting the data inside the Page Object, the test script remains focused purely on asserting the business logic against a clean array of strings."
        }
      ],
      "Medium": [
        {
          "objective": "arch_debug",
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "code": "class FormPage {\n  public submitBtn = this.page.locator('#submit');\n  async fillForm(data) { /* ... */ }\n}\n// In test.spec.ts\nawait formPage.fillForm(data);\nawait formPage.submitBtn.click();",
          "q": "Why is exposing physical DOM elements as public properties (`public submitBtn`) a structural leak?",
          "options": [
            "It violates TypeScript rules.",
            "It allows the test script to dictate *how* the action is performed. If the application changes and requires checking a 'Terms of Service' box before clicking submit, you have to update every test script. The Page Object should expose an action (`submit()`), not an element.",
            "It prevents the test from asserting.",
            "It is slower."
          ],
          "a": "It allows the test script to dictate *how* the action is performed. If the application changes and requires checking a 'Terms of Service' box before clicking submit, you have to update every test script. The Page Object should expose an action (`submit()`), not an element.",
          "explanation": "Encapsulation means hiding the internals. Exposing the button directly breaks the firewall. If the physical mechanics of submitting change, you have lost central control. (Note: Exposing locators strictly for *assertions* is acceptable, but not for *actions*)."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_debug",
          "type": "multi-select",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "scenario": "A developer argues that Page Objects should just return the `Locator` object so the test can use Playwright's Web-First assertions like `await expect(pageObj.successMessage()).toBeVisible()`.",
          "q": "Why is this actually the CORRECT architectural approach, rather than a leak? (Select all that apply)",
          "options": [
            "Because if the Page Object encapsulates the `expect` (e.g., `pageObj.verifySuccess()`), it steals the Test Intent from the spec file.",
            "Because returning a raw `Locator` preserves Playwright's lazy evaluation and auto-retrying mechanics.",
            "Because it breaks strict mode.",
            "Because it forces synchronous execution."
          ],
          "a": [
            "Because if the Page Object encapsulates the `expect` (e.g., `pageObj.verifySuccess()`), it steals the Test Intent from the spec file.",
            "Because returning a raw `Locator` preserves Playwright's lazy evaluation and auto-retrying mechanics."
          ],
          "explanation": "This is a critical nuance. Exposing a locator to *perform an action* is a leak. Exposing a locator to *perform an assertion* is perfect architecture. It keeps the physical selector hidden in the Page Object, but gives the assertion power back to the test runner, maximizing test readability and negative testing capabilities."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_debug",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "scenario": "Your application has an iframe containing a 3rd-party payment form. Your test reads: `await page.frameLocator('#stripe').locator('#cc').fill('1234');`.",
          "q": "If you are refactoring this to fix the leak, what should the architecture look like?",
          "options": [
            "Move the iframe logic into the `playwright.config.ts`.",
            "Create a `CheckoutPage` object that instantiates a `StripeComponent` object. The `StripeComponent` constructor takes the `frameLocator` as its boundary. The test simply calls `await checkout.stripe.fillCard('1234')`.",
            "Use XPath to avoid the iframe.",
            "Bypass the iframe using API."
          ],
          "a": "Create a `CheckoutPage` object that instantiates a `StripeComponent` object. The `StripeComponent` constructor takes the `frameLocator` as its boundary. The test simply calls `await checkout.stripe.fillCard('1234')`.",
          "explanation": "The test shouldn't know Stripe is an iframe. That is a massive architectural implementation detail. By hiding the iframe boundary logic entirely inside a Component Object, the test remains completely agnostic to the rendering strategy of the 3rd-party vendor."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_debug",
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "code": "test('Create Item', async ({ api, ui }) => {\n  const res = await api.post('/item', { name: 'Box' });\n  const id = (await res.json()).id;\n  await ui.goto(`/item/${id}`);\n});",
          "q": "While highly efficient, what abstraction is leaking from the API layer into the Test Layer in this snippet?",
          "options": [
            "None, this is perfect code.",
            "The HTTP protocol and JSON parsing logic. The test is manually unpacking the `res.json()`. A cleaner API Client abstraction would be `const id = await apiClient.createItem('Box');`, completely hiding the HTTP mechanics from the UI test.",
            "The UI navigation logic.",
            "The database schema."
          ],
          "a": "The HTTP protocol and JSON parsing logic. The test is manually unpacking the `res.json()`. A cleaner API Client abstraction would be `const id = await apiClient.createItem('Box');`, completely hiding the HTTP mechanics from the UI test.",
          "explanation": "Just as Page Objects hide the DOM (`locator.fill`), API Clients should hide the Network (`res.json()`). The test script should deal entirely in high-level business entities (Items, Users, IDs) and never see a raw HTTP response object."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_debug",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "scenario": "A test needs to handle a random promotional popup that appears 10% of the time.",
          "q": "Where is the most architecturally sound place to handle this non-deterministic UI element without leaking logic into 500 tests?",
          "options": [
            "Put an `if` statement in every test.",
            "Handle it in the Fixture Layer. Set up a global `page.route` or `page.addLocatorHandler` inside the `baseTest.ts` to automatically intercept and dismiss the promo before yielding the `page` to the tests.",
            "Ignore it and use `force: true`.",
            "Disable popups in the browser config."
          ],
          "a": "Handle it in the Fixture Layer. Set up a global `page.route` or `page.addLocatorHandler` inside the `baseTest.ts` to automatically intercept and dismiss the promo before yielding the `page` to the tests.",
          "explanation": "Global chaos requires global interception. If an element can appear anywhere, forcing every Page Object or Test to handle it is an unmaintainable leak. Playwright's `addLocatorHandler` (or fixture-level event listeners) creates an invisible shield that protects the entire suite."
        }
      ],
      "Hard": [
        {
          "objective": "arch_debug",
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "code": "class TableComponent {\n  async getRowTexts() {\n    const texts = await this.page.locator('tr').allInnerTexts();\n    return texts.map(t => t.split('\\t')[0]); // return only first column\n  }\n}",
          "q": "This component extracts data. However, it relies heavily on `\\t` (tab) characters injected by the browser's `innerText` engine. Why is this a dangerous leaky abstraction?",
          "options": [
            "Because tabs cannot be split.",
            "It ties the data parsing logic directly to the CSS rendering engine. If the developers change the CSS layout (e.g., using Grid or Flexbox), the `innerText` concatenation behavior changes, the `\\t` disappears, and the parser crashes, even though the data itself never changed.",
            "Because `innerText` is synchronous.",
            "Because Playwright disables tabs."
          ],
          "a": "It ties the data parsing logic directly to the CSS rendering engine. If the developers change the CSS layout (e.g., using Grid or Flexbox), the `innerText` concatenation behavior changes, the `\\t` disappears, and the parser crashes, even though the data itself never changed.",
          "explanation": "Parsing massive strings generated by `innerText` or `textContent` is highly brittle because you are relying on the browser's visual layout engine to format your data. The correct abstraction is to map over the physical DOM nodes (`tr`) and explicitly query the specific child node (`td.name`), decoupling the data extraction from the CSS layout."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_debug",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "scenario": "You have a `CheckoutPage` with a `pay()` method. Sometimes, the payment gateway is slow, and Playwright throws a `TimeoutError` inside the `pay()` method.",
          "q": "A developer wraps the *entire* test in a `try/catch` to handle this. Why is this an architectural failure, and where should the error handling reside?",
          "options": [
            "You should never handle errors.",
            "It's a failure because tests should fail immediately. If the external gateway is inherently unstable, the `CheckoutPage.pay()` method itself should implement a localized retry/polling loop around the API call, insulating the test script from the 3rd-party flakiness.",
            "The error should be handled in `playwright.config.ts`.",
            "The error should be caught by an ESLint rule."
          ],
          "a": "It's a failure because tests should fail immediately. If the external gateway is inherently unstable, the `CheckoutPage.pay()` method itself should implement a localized retry/polling loop around the API call, insulating the test script from the 3rd-party flakiness.",
          "explanation": "Flakiness mitigation must be pushed as deep down the stack as possible. If an external dependency is slow, the Page Object or API Client must absorb that latency via polling or internal retries. Leaking instability to the Test Layer forces developers to write defensive, convoluted test scripts."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_debug",
          "type": "multi-select",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "scenario": "You implement the State Transition pattern: `const dash = await loginPage.login()`. A junior developer complains that they now have to mock the backend API in every single test just to test the Dashboard, because `login()` actually attempts to hit the real network.",
          "q": "How do you plug this architectural leak using Dependency Injection? (Select all that apply)",
          "options": [
            "Pass a flag: `loginPage.login({ mock: true })`.",
            "Inject the API Client into the Page Object. The Fixture layer can provide a `RealApiClient` for E2E tests, and a `MockApiClient` for UI Component tests. The Page Object remains oblivious, simply calling `api.authenticate()`.",
            "Use Playwright's `page.route` in the Fixture layer to globally intercept the network request before the Page Object ever sends it.",
            "You cannot mock network in Playwright."
          ],
          "a": [
            "Inject the API Client into the Page Object. The Fixture layer can provide a `RealApiClient` for E2E tests, and a `MockApiClient` for UI Component tests. The Page Object remains oblivious, simply calling `api.authenticate()`.",
            "Use Playwright's `page.route` in the Fixture layer to globally intercept the network request before the Page Object ever sends it."
          ],
          "explanation": "A Page Object is tightly coupled to the network if it hardcodes real API expectations. By intercepting the network at the Fixture layer (via `page.route`) or injecting a mocked API interface, the Page Object remains pure, allowing you to instantly switch between slow E2E tests and lightning-fast mock tests without altering the abstraction."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_debug",
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "code": "async getInventoryCount(): Promise<number> {\n  const text = await this.page.locator('.count').innerText();\n  return parseInt(text.replace(' items', ''));\n}",
          "q": "What happens to the calling test script if the UI changes from `5 items` to `Out of Stock`?",
          "options": [
            "The test passes with `0`.",
            "The Page Object returns `NaN` (Not a Number). The test script receives this corrupted primitive and likely fails on a downstream assertion (`expect(NaN).toBeGreaterThan(0)`). The abstraction failed to validate its own internal parsing logic, leaking corrupted state to the test.",
            "Playwright throws a Strict Mode error.",
            "The browser crashes."
          ],
          "a": "The Page Object returns `NaN` (Not a Number). The test script receives this corrupted primitive and likely fails on a downstream assertion (`expect(NaN).toBeGreaterThan(0)`). The abstraction failed to validate its own internal parsing logic, leaking corrupted state to the test.",
          "explanation": "Robust abstractions must fail fast. If a Page Object attempts to parse data and the data is malformed, it must throw a clear, descriptive error *internally* (`throw new Error('Inventory UI missing numeric value')`). Returning `NaN` poisons the test script, making the root cause incredibly difficult to debug."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_debug",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "scenario": "Your framework uses Fixtures to create test data: `test('Edit', async ({ testUser }) => { ... })`. However, 5 tests in a row fail because the Database is temporarily locked, and the `testUser` fixture cannot create the user.",
          "q": "Why is Playwright's native Fixture Architecture structurally superior to a `beforeAll` block in handling this specific failure?",
          "options": [
            "Fixtures automatically unlock databases.",
            "If a `beforeAll` fails, the entire suite usually crashes or leaves tests in an indeterminate state. If a Fixture fails during initialization, Playwright surgically aborts only the specific tests that requested that exact dependency, explicitly marking them as 'Setup Failures' while allowing independent tests to continue running.",
            "Fixtures don't use databases.",
            "Fixtures run synchronously."
          ],
          "a": "If a `beforeAll` fails, the entire suite usually crashes or leaves tests in an indeterminate state. If a Fixture fails during initialization, Playwright surgically aborts only the specific tests that requested that exact dependency, explicitly marking them as 'Setup Failures' while allowing independent tests to continue running.",
          "explanation": "This represents the pinnacle of abstraction. Fixtures build a dependency graph. If Node C fails to initialize, Playwright prunes any Test that relies on Node C, but perfectly executes Tests that rely on Node A and B. This graph-based isolation prevents cascading failures, a massive upgrade over linear `beforeEach`/`beforeAll` architectures."
        }
      ]
    },
