  {
    "id": "61-arch-entity",
    "title": "12.4: Entity-Page Relationships",
    "url": "courses/playwright/61-arch-entity",
    "group": "🏗️ MODULE 12: ADVANCED ARCHITECTURE",
    "level": 9,
    "weight": 15,
    "meta": {
      "time": "20 mins",
      "type": "Architecture",
      "outcome": "Model business data independently from UI"
    },
    "objectives": [
      {
        "id": "entity_modeling",
        "title": "Create and use Entity models for test data"
      }
    ],
    "tasks": [],
    "quizMapping": [
      {
        "objective": "entity_modeling",
        "weight": 100
      }
    ],
    "spec": {
      "layer": "Entity",
      "standard": "Data Modeling"
    },
    "quizPool": {
      "Easy": [
        {
          "objective": "arch_entity",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "q": "How does utilizing strict 'Data Entities' catch this API schema change immediately at compile time?",
          "options": [
            "An HTML element.",
            "A JavaScript Object or Class that mathematically represents a core business concept (e.g., `User`, `Order`, `Product`), containing its raw data properties (ID, name, price) completely independent of the UI.",
            "A database connection string.",
            "A Playwright Fixture."
          ],
          "a": "A JavaScript Object or Class that mathematically represents a core business concept (e.g., `User`, `Order`, `Product`), containing its raw data properties (ID, name, price) completely independent of the UI.",
          "explanation": "Before interacting with the UI, enterprise tests generate 'Entities'. You don't just type random strings; you instantiate a `Product` entity with a known price, and then pass that entity through the framework to verify the UI matches the math.",
          "scenario": "Your tests pass random JSON objects directly into Page Objects. When the backend team renames 'firstName' to 'givenName', 100 tests silently start using undefined data."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_entity",
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "code": "// Approach A\nawait loginPage.login('admin@test.com', 'SecurePass123!');\n\n// Approach B\nawait loginPage.login(adminUserEntity);",
          "q": "Why is Approach B architecturally superior?",
          "options": [
            "It uses fewer characters.",
            "It couples the UI interaction directly to the Business Model. If the application changes to require a '2FA Token' for login, Approach A requires updating every string in every test. Approach B only requires adding the token to the `UserEntity` class; the test signatures remain unchanged.",
            "It makes the test run synchronously.",
            "Approach A is actually better."
          ],
          "a": "It couples the UI interaction directly to the Business Model. If the application changes to require a '2FA Token' for login, Approach A requires updating every string in every test. Approach B only requires adding the token to the `UserEntity` class; the test signatures remain unchanged.",
          "explanation": "Passing primitive strings creates brittle tests. Passing structured Entity Objects creates resilient APIs. The Page Object method unpacks the Entity (`entity.email`, `entity.password`) internally, insulating the test script from parameter bloat."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_entity",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "scenario": "You need to assert that the 'Order Summary' page displays the correct total price, tax, and shipping.",
          "q": "How should the Page Object interact with the Data Entity to perform this verification cleanly?",
          "options": [
            "The Page Object shouldn't use Entities.",
            "The test passes the `OrderEntity` into the Page Object: `await expect(summaryPage.totalText).toHaveText(orderEntity.calculateTotal())`.",
            "The Page Object connects to the database.",
            "The Page Object creates a new Entity."
          ],
          "a": "The test passes the `OrderEntity` into the Page Object: `await expect(summaryPage.totalText).toHaveText(orderEntity.calculateTotal())`.",
          "explanation": "This demonstrates the mathematical power of Entities. The `OrderEntity` encapsulates the business logic (`calculateTotal()`). The Test Layer calls that pure math function and compares it to the physical string extracted by the Page Object."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_entity",
          "type": "multi-select",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "scenario": "You are defining a `UserEntity` interface in TypeScript.",
          "q": "Which of the following properties belong in the Entity definition? (Select all that apply)",
          "options": [
            "emailAddress: string;",
            "passwordHash: string;",
            "loginButtonLocator: string;",
            "accountStatus: 'Active' | 'Suspended';"
          ],
          "a": [
            "emailAddress: string;",
            "passwordHash: string;",
            "accountStatus: 'Active' | 'Suspended';"
          ],
          "explanation": "Entities are purely data-driven business models. They must NEVER contain UI implementation details like locators or selectors. Mixing DOM logic into a Data Entity destroys the 4-Layer Separation."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_entity",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "scenario": "Your test creates a user via the UI, reads the success message, and finishes.",
          "q": "Why should the Page Object return a newly instantiated `UserEntity` instead of just returning `true`?",
          "options": [
            "It shouldn't.",
            "It allows the test to capture the state generated by the UI. If the UI generates a dynamic 'User ID: 999', the Page Object parses it, attaches it to the Entity, and returns it. The test can now use that ID for downstream API teardowns.",
            "It forces strict mode.",
            "It prevents TypeScript errors."
          ],
          "a": "It allows the test to capture the state generated by the UI. If the UI generates a dynamic 'User ID: 999', the Page Object parses it, attaches it to the Entity, and returns it. The test can now use that ID for downstream API teardowns.",
          "explanation": "Entities are the bridge between the UI and the API. If the UI creates data, it must be extracted and modeled into an Entity so the rest of the framework (like the database cleanup fixture) understands what was created."
        }
      ],
      "Medium": [
        {
          "objective": "arch_entity",
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "code": "async function createProduct(product: ProductEntity) {\n  await this.nameInput.fill(product.name);\n  if (product.isDiscounted) {\n    await this.discountCheckbox.check();\n  }\n  await this.submitBtn.click();\n}",
          "q": "What enterprise architectural pattern is demonstrated in this Page Object method?",
          "options": [
            "Data-Driven Execution. The execution path (whether to check the box) is dynamically dictated by the properties of the injected Entity. This allows one single method to handle dozens of different UI permutations based purely on the data model.",
            "A Leaky Abstraction.",
            "A Race Condition.",
            "Strict Mode Bypassing."
          ],
          "a": "Data-Driven Execution. The execution path (whether to check the box) is dynamically dictated by the properties of the injected Entity. This allows one single method to handle dozens of different UI permutations based purely on the data model.",
          "explanation": "Instead of writing `createNormalProduct()` and `createDiscountedProduct()`, you write one mathematically sound method that adapts to the Entity it receives. This drastically reduces Page Object bloat."
        },
        {
          "objective": "arch_entity",
          "type": "multi-select",
          "difficulty": "medium",
