            "It massively obscures underlying race conditions and performance degradations in the application code, creating a false sense of security.",
            "It drastically increases CI execution time, as failing tests must run 5 times to confirm failure.",
            "It breaks the Page Object Model.",
            "It disables telemetry."
          ],
          "a": [
            "It massively obscures underlying race conditions and performance degradations in the application code, creating a false sense of security.",
            "It drastically increases CI execution time, as failing tests must run 5 times to confirm failure."
          ],
          "explanation": "Retries are a band-aid, not a cure. If a test requires 3 retries to pass, the test (or the application) is broken. Enterprise pipelines should limit retries to 1 or 2 to handle genuine network blips, but rely on strict state transitions to eliminate actual flakiness."
        },
        {
          "objective": "anti_patterns",
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "code": "test('Verify Grid', async ({ page }) => {\n  const rowCount = await page.locator('.ag-row').count();\n  for (let i = 0; i < rowCount; i++) {\n    const rowText = await page.locator('.ag-row').nth(i).textContent();\n    expect(rowText).toContain('Active');\n  }\n});",
          "q": "What is the 'Looping Over Locators' Anti-Pattern, and why will this specifically crash in AG Grid?",
          "options": [
            "You cannot use `.nth()` in loops.",
            "It crashes due to DOM Virtualization. By resolving the `rowCount` synchronously, it assumes a static DOM. When it loops and scrolls, AG Grid destroys row 0 to render row 20. When the loop tries to access `.nth(0)` again, or if indices shift, the physical pointers are stale.",
            "The `expect` is synchronous.",
            "AG Grid does not use classes."
          ],
          "a": "It crashes due to DOM Virtualization. By resolving the `rowCount` synchronously, it assumes a static DOM. When it loops and scrolls, AG Grid destroys row 0 to render row 20. When the loop tries to access `.nth(0)` again, or if indices shift, the physical pointers are stale.",
          "explanation": "Looping over locators assumes the DOM is frozen in time. In modern SPAs, the DOM is highly volatile. To verify complex grids, you must extract data via the API, or execute a single `evaluateAll` script inside the browser console to gather data synchronously without CDP overhead."
        },
        {
          "objective": "anti_patterns",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "scenario": "A test verifies a successful file upload. The developer uses the 'UI-Only Teardown' Anti-Pattern: `afterAll(async () => { await page.click('#delete-file'); })`.",
          "q": "Why is UI-based teardown an enterprise architectural hazard?",
          "options": [
            "It isn't; it's recommended.",
            "If the test fails *because* the UI crashed or the upload failed, the UI is in an invalid state. The `afterAll` hook will then fail to find the `#delete-file` button, crashing the teardown process, and leaking the file permanently into the database. Teardown must ALWAYS be handled via the API layer.",
            "It breaks parallelization.",
            "The `afterAll` hook cannot access the page."
          ],
          "a": "If the test fails *because* the UI crashed or the upload failed, the UI is in an invalid state. The `afterAll` hook will then fail to find the `#delete-file` button, crashing the teardown process, and leaking the file permanently into the database. Teardown must ALWAYS be handled via the API layer.",
          "explanation": "Teardown mechanics must be completely decoupled from the system under test. If the UI is broken, you cannot use the UI to clean up the UI. The API Client layer must act as the omnipotent Garbage Collector, bypassing the UI entirely."
        }
      ]
    },
