          "a": "Because they only use the `request` fixture. API tests do not spawn a browser, do not render CSS, and do not execute JavaScript in a DOM. They are purely network-bound, meaning a single Node process can concurrently execute hundreds of them flawlessly.",
          "explanation": "UI parallelization is heavily CPU/RAM constrained by the Chromium binary. API parallelization is almost free. By grouping and fully-parallelizing API suites, you can achieve execution speeds that look like load testing."
