    "quizPool": {
      "Easy": [
        {
          "objective": "adv_assertions",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "q": "How can extending `expect` improve the readability and domain-specificity of these assertions?",
          "options": [
            "To make assertions run faster.",
            "To encapsulate complex, multi-step validation logic into a single, highly readable assertion (e.g., `expect(page).toBeInAdminState()`), making the test script purely declarative.",
            "To bypass strict mode.",
            "To assert against database schemas."
          ],
          "a": "To encapsulate complex, multi-step validation logic into a single, highly readable assertion (e.g., `expect(page).toBeInAdminState()`), making the test script purely declarative.",
          "explanation": "If a test has to perform 5 assertions to verify an 'Admin State' (checking URL, checking a header, checking a specific cookie), you are leaking implementation details into the test. A Custom Matcher wraps those 5 checks into one domain-specific business assertion.",
          "scenario": "You find yourself writing `expect(response.status()).toBe(200); expect(response.ok()).toBeTruthy();` in 50 different API tests."
        },
        {
          "objective": "adv_assertions",
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "code": "expect.extend({\n  toBeWithinRange(received: number, floor: number, ceiling: number) {\n    const pass = received >= floor && received <= ceiling;\n    return { pass, message: () => `Expected ${received} to be within ${floor}-${ceiling}` };\n  }\n});",
          "q": "In this custom matcher, what specific object format must be returned to interface with Playwright's assertion engine?",
          "options": [
            "A boolean (true or false).",
            "An object containing exactly two properties: a boolean `pass` (whether the assertion succeeded), and a `message` function (returning the error string if it failed).",
            "A Promise.",
            "A JSON string."
          ],
          "a": "An object containing exactly two properties: a boolean `pass` (whether the assertion succeeded), and a `message` function (returning the error string if it failed).",
          "explanation": "This is the underlying contract of the `expect` engine. You cannot just return `true`. You must return the mathematical outcome (`pass`) and the human-readable explanation (`message`) so the Playwright HTML report can display exactly why it failed."
        },
        {
          "objective": "adv_assertions",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "scenario": "You write a custom matcher `toHavePrice(locator, expectedPrice)`. It works perfectly in `expect(loc).toHavePrice(10)`. However, a developer writes `expect(loc).not.toHavePrice(10)` and the error message is highly confusing.",
          "q": "How does the custom matcher know if the developer used the `.not` modifier?",
          "options": [
            "It doesn't.",
            "The `this` context inside the matcher contains a `this.isNot` boolean. Your `message` function must dynamically read `this.isNot` to return either 'Expected X to have price Y' or 'Expected X NOT to have price Y'.",
            "You must write a separate `notToHavePrice` matcher.",
            "Playwright automatically inverses the string."
          ],
