          "scenario": "A specific E2E test genuinely requires 2 minutes to complete (e.g., uploading a massive video file). The global timeout is set to 30 seconds, so the test always fails.",
          "q": "How do you architecturally handle this specific outlier without increasing the global timeout for all 500 tests?",
          "options": [
            "Change the global timeout to 2 minutes.",
            "Add `test.setTimeout(120000)` or use the `test.slow()` annotation at the top of the test. `test.slow()` inherently multiplies the global timeout for that specific test block by 3x, gracefully absorbing the latency without making the entire suite lethargic.",
            "Use `page.waitForTimeout`.",
            "Disable timeouts entirely."
          ],
          "a": "Add `test.setTimeout(120000)` or use the `test.slow()` annotation at the top of the test. `test.slow()` inherently multiplies the global timeout for that specific test block by 3x, gracefully absorbing the latency without making the entire suite lethargic.",
          "explanation": "Global timeouts must be aggressively short (30s) to kill hanging tests instantly. When you increase the global timeout to accommodate one slow test, you destroy the pipeline's fail-fast mechanism. Localized timeout overriding (`test.slow()`) preserves strict global boundaries."
        },
        {
          "objective": "tags_annotations",
