          "difficulty": "medium",
          "code": "test.fail('Submit without terms', async ({ page }) => {\n  await page.click('#submit');\n  await expect(page.locator('.error')).toBeVisible();\n});",
          "q": "A junior developer uses `test.fail()` here because they are testing a failure state. Why is this a fundamental misunderstanding of the Playwright architecture?",
          "options": [
            "The code is perfect.",
            "`test.fail()` does not mean 'test a negative scenario'. It literally tells Playwright: 'I expect this Node.js script to throw a fatal error. If it executes successfully without crashing, FAIL the test'. Negative scenario tests should just use standard `test()` with negative assertions (`not.toBeVisible`).",
            "It skips the test.",
            "It forces strict mode."
          ],
          "a": "`test.fail()` does not mean 'test a negative scenario'. It literally tells Playwright: 'I expect this Node.js script to throw a fatal error. If it executes successfully without crashing, FAIL the test'. Negative scenario tests should just use standard `test()` with negative assertions (`not.toBeVisible`).",
          "explanation": "This is a massive conceptual trap. `test.fail()` is used exclusively when you know a bug exists in the application, you write a test exposing the bug, and you want the CI to be Green *until* the developers fix the bug (at which point the test will unexpectedly pass, failing the CI to alert you to remove the `fail` tag)."
        },
        {
          "objective": "tags_annotations",
