        {
          "objective": "tags_annotations",
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "code": "test.describe('Payment Gateway', () => {\n  test.fail(({ browserName }) => browserName === 'firefox', 'Firefox has known SSL issue');\n\n  test('Process Card', async ({ page }) => { ... });\n  test('Refund', async ({ page }) => { ... });\n});",
          "q": "What happens when this entire describe block is executed on Firefox?",
          "options": [
            "It skips the tests.",
            "Playwright actively executes both tests. If they pass, Playwright instantly marks the suite as FAILED (because a `test.fail` was expected to throw). If they crash, Playwright marks the suite as PASSED. It perfectly models expected technical debt.",
            "It crashes the runner.",
            "It disables strict mode."
          ],
          "a": "Playwright actively executes both tests. If they pass, Playwright instantly marks the suite as FAILED (because a `test.fail` was expected to throw). If they crash, Playwright marks the suite as PASSED. It perfectly models expected technical debt.",
          "explanation": "Placing `test.fail` at the `describe` level cascades the expectation. This is incredibly powerful for tracking browser-specific bugs. The CI remains 'Green' while the bug exists, but the moment the vendor fixes the SSL issue and the test passes, the pipeline goes Red, notifying the team to remove the technical debt flag."
        },
        {
          "objective": "tags_annotations",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "scenario": "Your CI pipeline runs `npx playwright test --project=setup` and then `npx playwright test --grep @smoke`. The setup project generates the `storageState.json` file.",
          "q": "Why does running the `--grep` command in a completely separate CLI invocation structurally break the Project Dependencies architecture?",
          "options": [
            "It doesn't break it.",
            "When you split execution into separate CLI runs, Playwright's DAG resolver is bypassed. If the `setup` project fails, the CI server will still mindlessly execute the second `grep` command. You must run `npx playwright test --grep @smoke`, and let Playwright natively resolve and execute the setup dependency first.",
            "It causes the JSON file to delete.",
            "It ignores the grep."
          ],
          "a": "When you split execution into separate CLI runs, Playwright's DAG resolver is bypassed. If the `setup` project fails, the CI server will still mindlessly execute the second `grep` command. You must run `npx playwright test --grep @smoke`, and let Playwright natively resolve and execute the setup dependency first.",
          "explanation": "Never orchestrate Playwright execution in bash/yaml scripts. Playwright is a graph engine. Tell it what you want (`--grep @smoke`), and let its internal dependency resolver guarantee that the prerequisite `setup` project is executed perfectly before evaluating the target nodes."
        }
      ]
    },
