          "difficulty": "hard",
          "code": "test('Poll Status', async ({ request }) => {\n  await expect.poll(async () => {\n    const res = await request.get('/api/status/123');\n    return (await res.json()).state;\n  }).toBe('READY');\n});",
          "q": "Why is combining `expect.poll` with the `request` fixture an elite architectural pattern for testing asynchronous backend jobs?",
          "options": [
            "It is not; it is an anti-pattern.",
            "It eliminates manual `while` loops and `sleep()` commands. Many APIs return 'PENDING' while a background worker processes data. `expect.poll` mathematically retries the API call natively, evaluating the JSON response until the state shifts to 'READY', drastically optimizing test speed and stability.",
            "It forces the API to run faster.",
            "It bypasses authentication."
          ],
          "a": "It eliminates manual `while` loops and `sleep()` commands. Many APIs return 'PENDING' while a background worker processes data. `expect.poll` mathematically retries the API call natively, evaluating the JSON response until the state shifts to 'READY', drastically optimizing test speed and stability.",
          "explanation": "Asynchronous backend queues (Kafka, RabbitMQ, SQS) are the bane of E2E testing. Polling the status API is the only deterministic way to synchronize the test script with the eventual consistency of the backend architecture."
