          "a": "It allows an assertion to fail WITHOUT instantly terminating the test execution. The test will continue to run subsequent steps and assertions, but will ultimately be marked as 'Failed' at the very end.",
          "explanation": "Standard assertions (`expect()`) are hard stops. If one fails, the test dies immediately. `expect.soft()` gathers intelligence. It collects failures like a shopping cart, reporting all of them at the end.",
          "scenario": "You are validating a large data table. If one row's data is incorrect, you still want to check the remaining 99 rows before failing the test."
        },
        {
