          ],
          "a": "It will show TWO distinct errors (one for `#name`, one for `#age`) stacked within the same test result. This prevents the 'Whack-a-Mole' debugging cycle.",
          "explanation": "Without soft assertions, you fix `#name`, re-run CI (wait 10 minutes), discover `#age` is broken, fix `#age`, re-run CI (wait 10 minutes). Soft assertions expose the complete blast radius of a bug in a single run."
        },
        {
          "objective": "data_debug",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "scenario": "You are writing a setup test that must click 'Login'. If the login button is missing, the entire rest of the test (checking profile, changing settings) is physically impossible.",
          "q": "Should you use `expect.soft()` to verify the login button is visible?",
          "options": [
            "Yes, always use soft assertions.",
            "No. You MUST use a hard `expect()`. If a prerequisite action is impossible, continuing the test will just generate a massive cascade of meaningless 'Element Not Found' errors for the next 30 lines of code. Fail fast.",
