    "quizPool": {
      "Easy": [
        {
          "objective": "capstone_pom",
          "type": "mcq",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "q": "In an Enterprise Capstone project, what is the primary structural purpose of a Page Object Model (POM)?",
          "options": [
            "To hold database connection strings.",
            "To physically separate the brittle UI details (CSS/XPath locators) from the declarative business logic (Test scripts), creating a centralized, highly maintainable interface for interacting with the application.",
            "To make the code look object-oriented.",
            "To write assertions."
          ],
          "a": "To physically separate the brittle UI details (CSS/XPath locators) from the declarative business logic (Test scripts), creating a centralized, highly maintainable interface for interacting with the application.",
          "explanation": "If a UI button ID changes from `btn-submit` to `btn-save`, you should only have to update ONE line of code in the POM, instantly fixing the 500 tests that use that button."
        },
        {
          "objective": "capstone_pom",
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "code": "class LoginPage {\n  constructor(public page: Page) {\n    this.username = page.locator('#user');\n  }\n  async login() {\n    await this.username.fill('admin');\n  }\n}",
          "q": "Why is hardcoding the string 'admin' inside the `login` method a fundamental violation of Capstone POM design?",
          "options": [
            "It is a spelling error.",
            "The POM should define ACTIONS, not DATA. The method should accept parameters (`async login(user: string)`). Hardcoding data inside the POM destroys its reusability, forcing you to create redundant methods like `loginAsAdmin()`, `loginAsViewer()`, etc.",
            "It uses `async`.",
            "The `fill` method is deprecated."
          ],
          "a": "The POM should define ACTIONS, not DATA. The method should accept parameters (`async login(user: string)`). Hardcoding data inside the POM destroys its reusability, forcing you to create redundant methods like `loginAsAdmin()`, `loginAsViewer()`, etc.",
          "explanation": "A Page Object is a dumb robot. It knows HOW to click the keyboard, but the test script must tell it WHAT to type."
        },
        {
          "objective": "capstone_pom",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "scenario": "Your Capstone application has a persistent 'Sidebar Navigation' menu that is physically present on every single page.",
          "q": "How do you architecturally model this in your POM structure to avoid duplicating the sidebar locators 50 times?",
          "options": [
            "Copy and paste the locators into every Page class.",
            "Create a dedicated `SidebarComponent` class. You can either instantiate this component globally as a Fixture, or instantiate it as a property inside a `BasePage` class that all other Page Objects inherit from (Composition or Inheritance).",
            "Do not test the sidebar.",
            "Use a global variable."
          ],
          "a": "Create a dedicated `SidebarComponent` class. You can either instantiate this component globally as a Fixture, or instantiate it as a property inside a `BasePage` class that all other Page Objects inherit from (Composition or Inheritance).",
          "explanation": "DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) applies to architecture. If a UI component is shared across the application, its mathematical representation (the POM) must be shared across the framework."
        },
        {
          "objective": "capstone_pom",
          "type": "multi-select",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "scenario": "You are building the POM for a complex Data Grid (table).",
          "q": "What should the POM methods return to the test script? (Select all that apply)",
          "options": [
            "Raw strings containing HTML.",
            "Playwright `Locator` objects, allowing the test script to run standard Web-First Assertions (`expect(pom.table).toBeVisible()`).",
            "Booleans representing success or failure.",
            "Specific data extractions (e.g., `async getRowText(index): Promise<string>`) so the test can assert against the pure data."
          ],
          "a": [
            "Playwright `Locator` objects, allowing the test script to run standard Web-First Assertions (`expect(pom.table).toBeVisible()`).",
            "Specific data extractions (e.g., `async getRowText(index): Promise<string>`) so the test can assert against the pure data."
          ],
          "explanation": "A POM has two primary outputs: 1) Exposing the raw Locators for standard framework assertions, and 2) Providing specialized data extraction methods to hide complex DOM traversal from the test script."
        },
        {
          "objective": "capstone_pom",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "scenario": "A Junior QA writes: `expect(await loginPage.isLoggedIn()).toBe(true);` inside the test, and puts the `expect().toBeVisible()` inside the `isLoggedIn` POM method.",
          "q": "Why will the Lead Architect reject this PR immediately?",
          "options": [
            "Because `true` is not a string.",
            "Because it violates the 'No Assertions in the POM' rule. If the assertion fails inside the POM, the error trace becomes opaque. The test script must contain the assertions to maintain clear, declarative reporting.",
            "Because POMs cannot return booleans.",
            "Because it should use `setTimeout`."
          ],
          "a": "Because it violates the 'No Assertions in the POM' rule. If the assertion fails inside the POM, the error trace becomes opaque. The test script must contain the assertions to maintain clear, declarative reporting.",
          "explanation": "The Test file is the judge. The POM file is the lawyer. The lawyer presents the evidence (locators, text), but the judge makes the final ruling (expect)."
        }
      ],
      "Medium": [
        {
          "objective": "capstone_pom",
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "code": "class DashboardPage {\n  public header = this.page.locator('.header');\n  constructor(public page: Page) {}\n}",
          "q": "Why is initializing a locator as a class property OUTSIDE the constructor a dangerous anti-pattern in TypeScript/Playwright?",
          "options": [
            "It uses too much memory.",
            "It creates an execution order bug. The property initializer runs BEFORE the constructor assigns `this.page`. It will crash instantly with `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'locator')`.",
            "It is perfectly fine.",
