    "quizPool": {
      "Easy": [
        {
          "objective": "docker_ci",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "q": "How does running Playwright inside a Docker container solve this 'Works on my machine' problem?",
          "options": [
            "To make the code harder to read.",
            "To achieve mathematically perfect 'Environment Parity'. A Docker container guarantees that the exact same Linux OS, Node.js version, browser binaries, and system fonts are used by every developer and the CI server, entirely eliminating the 'it works on my machine' paradox.",
            "To bypass authentication.",
            "To write Python scripts."
          ],
          "a": "To achieve mathematically perfect 'Environment Parity'. A Docker container guarantees that the exact same Linux OS, Node.js version, browser binaries, and system fonts are used by every developer and the CI server, entirely eliminating the 'it works on my machine' paradox.",
          "explanation": "Flakiness is often an OS mismatch. A font renders differently on a Mac than on Ubuntu, breaking a visual test. Docker freezes the physical reality of the execution environment into an immutable image.",
          "scenario": "Tests pass perfectly on a Mac laptop, but fail on an Ubuntu CI server because the system fonts render slightly differently, causing Visual Regression mismatches."
        },
        {
          "objective": "docker_ci",
          "type": "code-analysis",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "code": "FROM mcr.microsoft.com/playwright:v1.40.0-jammy\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY package.json .\nRUN npm install\nCOPY . .\nCMD [\"npx\", \"playwright\", \"test\"]",
          "q": "Why is it absolutely critical to use the official Microsoft Playwright image (`mcr.microsoft.com/playwright`) as the `FROM` base, instead of a standard `node:18` image?",
          "options": [
            "It is smaller.",
            "The official image comes pre-installed with all the massive, complex Linux OS dependencies (libnss3, libasound2, fonts, xvfb) required to boot Chromium, WebKit, and Firefox in a headless server environment. A standard node image lacks these, and the browsers will instantly crash.",
            "It is written in TypeScript.",
            "It uses less CPU."
          ],
          "a": "The official image comes pre-installed with all the massive, complex Linux OS dependencies (libnss3, libasound2, fonts, xvfb) required to boot Chromium, WebKit, and Firefox in a headless server environment. A standard node image lacks these, and the browsers will instantly crash.",
          "explanation": "Headless browsers are incredibly complex beasts. They require graphics libraries, audio drivers, and memory handlers that do not exist on standard web-server Docker images."
        },
        {
          "objective": "docker_ci",
          "type": "scenario",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "scenario": "You are running Playwright tests via Docker locally, and a test fails. You want to view the `playwright-report`.",
          "q": "What Docker concept MUST you configure to ensure you can actually see the report on your laptop?",
          "options": [
            "Docker Push.",
            "Volume Mapping (e.g., `-v ${PWD}/playwright-report:/app/playwright-report`). Because the Docker container is a sealed box that is destroyed after the tests finish, you must map the internal report folder to a physical folder on your laptop's hard drive to 'rescue' the files.",
            "Port Forwarding.",
            "Docker Exec."
          ],
          "a": "Volume Mapping (e.g., `-v ${PWD}/playwright-report:/app/playwright-report`). Because the Docker container is a sealed box that is destroyed after the tests finish, you must map the internal report folder to a physical folder on your laptop's hard drive to 'rescue' the files.",
          "explanation": "A container is a black hole. If you don't explicitly poke a hole (Volume) for the output data to escape, the data is annihilated when the container dies."
        },
        {
          "objective": "docker_ci",
          "type": "multi-select",
          "difficulty": "easy",
          "scenario": "You are migrating a legacy Selenium framework to Playwright.",
          "q": "What architectural benefits does Playwright's containerization strategy offer over Selenium Grid? (Select all that apply)",
